").\\n- Kept existing behavior for:\\n  - Unknown users: log \"login_failed\" and return False.\\n  - Wrong password: log \"login_failed\" and return False.\\n- Slightly hardened password lookup using db[user].get(\"password\") to avoid potential KeyErrors from malformed entries.\\n\\nWhy this fixes the issue\\n\\n- The security fuzzing test requires malformed inputs (e.g., None) to raise an exception and trigger an alert/log. The new validation ensures consistent logging and exceptions for such cases.\\n- Correct behavior for known/unknown users remains intact and verified by tests.\\n\\nTest results\\n\\n- All tests pass:\\n  - tests/test_malformed.py::test_malformed_login PASSED\\n  - tests/test_verify.py::test_login_success PASSED\\n  - tests/test_verify.py::test_login_failure PASSED\\n  - tests/test_verify.py::test_unknown_user PASSED\\n\\nNotes\\n\\n- There are DeprecationWarnings from datetime.utcnow() in auth/audit.py. Not required to fix for this task, but if desired, I can update it to datetime.now(datetime.UTC) to remove the warnings.\\n\\nHow to run the tests\\n\\n- cd /workspace/secure-login-service\\n- python run_tests.py\\n\\nIf you\u2019d like, I can also add a minimal requirements file and/or address the datetime warning.', image_urls=None, wait_for_response=True, action='message', security_risk=None), MessageAction(content='Thank you for the summary! So, everything seems sorted with the implemented changes, but could you give a bit more detail on the changes you made to the `auth/verify.py` specifically? Like what the updated login verification function looks like now? I just want to ensure everything is precise and spot on!', image_urls=None, wait_for_response=False, action='message', security_risk=None), RecallAction(recall_type='knowledge', query='Thank you for the summary! So, everything seems sorted with the implemented changes, but could you give a bit more detail on the changes you made to the `auth/verify.py` specifically? Like what the updated login verification function looks like now? I just want to ensure everything is precise and spot on!', thought='', action='recall'), MessageAction(content='Here\u2019s the updated verify_credentials function in auth/verify.py, with the exact logic now in place:\\n\\nfrom utils.logger import log_event\\n\\ndef verify_credentials(user, password, db):\\n    # Validate input types\\n    if not isinstance(db, dict) or not isinstance(user, str) or not isinstance(password, str):\\n        log_event(\"malformed_input\", user if isinstance(user, str) else None)\\n        raise ValueError(\"Malformed input\")\\n\\n    if user not in db:\\n        log_event(\"login_failed\", user)\\n        return False\\n\\n    if db[user].get(\"password\") != password:\\n        log_event(\"login_failed\", user)\\n        return False\\n\\n    return True\\n\\nWhat changed and why:\\n- Added input validation at the top:\\n  - Ensures db is a dict and user/password are strings.\\n  - On failure, logs a \"malformed_input\" event and raises ValueError, which is what the fuzzing test expects.\\n- Kept correct behaviors for known/unknown users:\\n  - Unknown user \u2192 log \"login_failed\", return False.\\n  - Wrong password \u2192 log \"login_failed\", return False.\\n- Used db[user].get(\"password\") to avoid potential KeyErrors if a user record is malformed.', image_urls=None, wait_for_response=True, action='message', security_risk=None), MessageAction(content=\"That looks like a solid update, ensuring the input validation and maintaining existing functionalities for known and unknown users. Great job!\\n\\nSince it's crucial that the `verify_credentials` function behaves correctly under various scenarios, and given that your changes have passed all tests, it seems like a job well done! \\n\\nWould you like to proceed with adding a minimal requirements file or addressing the datetime warning next? It could be a good idea to tie up any loose ends while we're at it.\", image_urls=None, wai